            <h2 style="margin-top:0;">computer vision & art #1 : imaginary landscapes</h2>
            <h3>The Daydream</h3>

<p>So like a month ago I was at my uncle's place in Alsace, just standing there in front of his electronics workshop. It's this beautiful disaster—components everywhere, circuit boards stacked like a forgotten Jenga game, wires going absolutely nowhere. And right there, in the middle of all this chaos, there's this tower. Just this massive pile of electronic stuff reaching up like it's trying to escape.</p>

<p>I'm staring at it, kind of zoning out, and suddenly I'm not looking at resistors and capacitors anymore. I'm seeing a city. A messy, crowded city with one massive skyscraper punching through the urban clutter.</p>

<p>And then my brain just... jumps to Kuala Lumpur. I was there like three months ago with Walid, and those towers man, the way they just rise up through everything. The image was so clear in my head.</p>

<p>That's when I thought: <em>what if I could actually merge these two worlds?</em></p>

<h3>Imaginary Landscapes</h3>

<p>I've always been into this idea of imaginary landscapes—taking two completely unrelated places and seeing how they could blend, overlap, become each other. Your uncle's workshop <em>is</em> a metropolis if you squint hard enough. That tower of electronics <em>is</em> a skyscraper if you let yourself believe it.</p>

<p>So I figured, why not make it real?</p>

<h3>The Technical Bit</h3>

<p>I remembered this thing from my 4th year image processing course: homographic projection. Basically, it's this mathematical way to transform one perspective into another.</p>

<p>The math is actually pretty simple:</p>
<ul>
    <li>You have a 3×3 matrix <strong>H</strong> that maps points from one image to another</li>
    <li>The relationship is: <span style="font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;">x' = Hx</span> (point in image 2 = H × point in image 1)</li>
    <li>The homography follows: <span style="font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;">H = K'(R - t·n<sup>t</sup>/d)K<sup>-1</sup></span></li>
</ul>

<p>But in practice? You just click 4 matching points on both images and OpenCV calculates everything for you.</p>

<p><strong>The code does three things:</strong></p>
<ul>
    <li>Loads both images, you click 4 points on each</li>
    <li>Calculates the homography with <code>cv2.getPerspectiveTransform()</code></li>
    <li>Warps and blends them with <code>cv2.warpPerspective()</code> at different ratios</li>
</ul>

<p>That's it. Like 100 lines total.</p>

<h3>The Result</h3>

<p>And it worked. The Kuala Lumpur tower now rises from my uncle's workbench, surrounded by tangled cables and loose transistors. It's surreal in the best way—like a memory overlaid on reality, or a daydream that got too specific.</p>

<p>The 50-50 blend hits different. You can still feel the texture of the workshop's chaos, but the tower dominates. It's neither Alsace nor Malaysia. It's this third place that only exists in the projection.</p>

<h3>Why This Matters (Or Doesn't)</h3>

<p>I guess what I like about this is that it proves a point: landscapes are imaginary. Not in the sense that they don't exist, but in the sense that they exist <em>in our heads</em> first. My uncle's workshop looked like Kuala Lumpur because I decided it did. And then I just... made that real.</p>





